Mars once&nbsp;had active volcanoes, like Olympus Mons, which is three times higher than Everest, and so massive that when it first emerged, it started to sink back into the planet.&nbsp;
A new discovery raises the possibility Mars' volcanoes aren't so dead after all.&nbsp;
The inner solar system is brimming with volcanic fire. Earth has around 40 volcanoes erupting at any one moment. And scientists generally agree that Venus, despite being shrouded in thick clouds, has its own lava-spewing mountains. But Mars, once covered in active volcanoes, is now silent. Nobody has ever seen any definitive signs of volcanic activity there.
But that doesn’t mean that Mars is volcanically dead—and some tantalizing new evidence suggests we shouldn’t count it out just yet.
